# Contract-test credentials — copy to ops/contract-tests.env and fill in.
#
#   cp ops/contract-tests.env.example ops/contract-tests.env
#   chmod 600 ops/contract-tests.env
#   docker compose --profile contract up -d
#
# ops/contract-tests.env is gitignored. Keep it that way.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RULES, from DRIFT-AND-OSS-PLAN.md. These are not style preferences.
#
#   1. NEVER grant a test credential a destructive scope. These tests verify that BlastRadius
#      parses a scope correctly; they do not need the power to delete anything, and a CI box
#      holding a credential that can drop a database is the exact failure this tool describes.
#   2. Use a DEDICATED account per provider, never a personal one. An orphaned personal
#      credential becomes a permanent red CI run that everyone learns to ignore.
#   3. EXPECT values are the assertion. If a run fails on a scope diff, the question is
#      "did the provider change, or did someone edit the credential?" — and those look
#      identical unless you write the expectation down here.
#
# The pairing is BLASTRADIUS_TEST_<ID> (the credential) and BLASTRADIUS_EXPECT_<ID> (a JSON
# array of the scope strings it should yield). A provider with no TEST value is skipped.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- GitHub ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Classic PAT. Do NOT use a fine-grained PAT: those return no scope header, which the module
# correctly reports as unresolved, so they would assert nothing.
#
# Tick THREE read-only scopes, not one: read:user, read:org, read:packages.
#
# One scope proves only that a single string survives the round trip. Three prove that the
# comma-separated x-oauth-scopes header is split and trimmed correctly against the live API —
# and multi-scope parsing is the path every real credential actually takes, so testing only
# the single-scope case leaves the common path unverified.
#
# All three are read-only by design. This does NOT weaken the rule that a test credential must
# never hold a destructive scope: breadth here is horizontal, across harmless scopes, not
# vertical toward dangerous ones. delete_repo and admin:org can never be tested live, and
# their mapping is covered exhaustively offline in test/unit/providers-phase1.test.mjs
# instead, because toCapabilities is pure and needs no credential.
#
# Set EXPECT from what GitHub actually returns, not from what you ticked — it normalises the
# set and may reorder or imply scopes. Run the contract test and read the diff on failure.
BLASTRADIUS_TEST_GITHUB=
BLASTRADIUS_EXPECT_GITHUB=["read:user","read:org","read:packages"]

# --- Stripe ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A TEST-MODE key (sk_test_...). Never a live key.
# The module reports mode and access class separately, so both strings are expected. For a
# restricted test key (rk_test_) use ["mode:test","restricted"] instead.
BLASTRADIUS_TEST_STRIPE=
BLASTRADIUS_EXPECT_STRIPE=["mode:test","full-access"]

# --- Railway -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Create at https://railway.com/account/tokens with the workspace dropdown set to
# "No workspace" — that is what makes it an ACCOUNT token. Workspace and project tokens
# cannot answer the `me` query the module uses, so they will fail this test.
#
# Use a THROWAWAY account with no projects. Railway has no token scopes whatsoever, so an
# account token issued on an account you actually use can delete every volume in every
# workspace you belong to. There is no read-only option to fall back on.
#
# Costs nothing in practice: the test deploys nothing, so an empty account stays within the
# free plan's credit. A card is required at signup.
BLASTRADIUS_TEST_RAILWAY=
BLASTRADIUS_EXPECT_RAILWAY=["account"]

# --- Supabase ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Use the PUBLISHABLE key (sb_publishable_...) from a throwaway project. Dashboard →
# Project Settings → API Keys → "Publishable and secret API keys" tab. It is designed to be
# public, so it is the safe one to hold here.
#
# NOT an anon key: projects created since November 2025 do not get legacy anon/service_role
# keys at all, and those are scheduled for removal in late 2026. NOT a secret key
# (sb_secret_) — it bypasses row-level security. NOT a management token (sbp_) — it can
# delete every project in the account.
#
# Be aware what this test does and does not prove: Supabase classification is entirely
# offline (prefix and JWT claims), so this asserts our own parsing, not the live API. It
# pins the key format; it cannot detect provider drift. Supabase's real drift coverage is
# the changelog scanner, which works well for them. See DRIFT-AND-OSS-PLAN.md §3.
BLASTRADIUS_TEST_SUPABASE=
BLASTRADIUS_EXPECT_SUPABASE=["publishable"]

# --- Vercel ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hobby-tier token on a throwaway account. Vercel offers no project-level scoping, so this
# credential reaches everything that account owns — keep it owning nothing.
BLASTRADIUS_TEST_VERCEL=
BLASTRADIUS_EXPECT_VERCEL=["account"]

# --- Cloudflare --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A minimal-permission API token. The module reports every live Cloudflare token as
# unresolved (the verify endpoint returns status, not scopes), so EXPECT is empty and the
# assertion is that the token still verifies as active at all. If Cloudflare ever exposes
# token policies, this test is what will tell us.
BLASTRADIUS_TEST_CLOUDFLARE=
BLASTRADIUS_EXPECT_CLOUDFLARE=[]
